1
00:00:06,832 --> 00:00:15,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the suttas I'll read right now are concerned with thinking and thoughts.

2
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:21,904
[SPEAKER_01]: The first sutta is in the Majjhima Nikaya, number 19, and it's called Two Kinds of Thought.

3
00:00:25,968 --> 00:00:29,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus have I heard, on one occasion, a blessed one was living in savarthi.

4
00:00:30,311 --> 00:00:31,712
[SPEAKER_01]: There he addressed the bhikkhus thus.

5
00:00:32,573 --> 00:00:34,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhikkhus, venerable sir, they were glad.

6
00:00:35,579 --> 00:00:49,488
[SPEAKER_01]: The Blessed One said this, because before my enlightenment, while I was still only an unenlightened bodhisattva, it occurred to me, suppose that I divide my thoughts into two classes.

7
00:00:49,528 --> 00:01:02,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I set on one side thoughts of sensual desire, thoughts of ill-will, thoughts of cruelty, and set on the other side thoughts of renunciation, thoughts of non-ill-will, and thoughts of non-cruelty.

8
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:10,990
[SPEAKER_01]: As I abided thus, diligent, ardent, and resolute, a thought of sensual desire arose in me.

9
00:01:12,411 --> 00:01:13,911
[SPEAKER_01]: I understood thus.

10
00:01:13,971 --> 00:01:16,492
[SPEAKER_01]: This thought of sensual desire has arisen in me.

11
00:01:17,412 --> 00:01:22,313
[SPEAKER_01]: This leads to my own affliction, to the other's affliction, and to the affliction of both.

12
00:01:23,193 --> 00:01:28,074
[SPEAKER_01]: It obstructs wisdom, causes difficulties, and leads away from nibbāna.

13
00:01:29,034 --> 00:01:33,095
[SPEAKER_01]: When I considered this leads to my own affliction, it subsided in me.

14
00:01:33,916 --> 00:01:38,700
[SPEAKER_01]: When I considered this leads to others' affliction, it subsided in me.

15
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:43,845
[SPEAKER_01]: When I considered this leads to the affliction of both, it subsided in me.

16
00:01:44,826 --> 00:01:52,133
[SPEAKER_01]: When I considered this obstructs wisdom, causes difficulties, and leads away from nibbāna, it subsided in me.

17
00:01:53,214 --> 00:01:59,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever a thought of sensual desire arose in me, I abandoned it, removed it, and did away with it.

18
00:02:01,709 --> 00:02:07,153
[SPEAKER_01]: As I abided thus, diligent, ardent, and resolute, a thought of ill-will arose in me.

19
00:02:08,234 --> 00:02:09,955
[SPEAKER_01]: A thought of cruelty arose in me.

20
00:02:11,557 --> 00:02:12,878
[SPEAKER_01]: I understood thus.

21
00:02:12,918 --> 00:02:15,099
[SPEAKER_01]: This thought of cruelty has arisen in me.

22
00:02:15,380 --> 00:02:19,943
[SPEAKER_01]: This leads to my own affliction, to others' affliction, and to the affliction of both.

23
00:02:20,604 --> 00:02:24,907
[SPEAKER_01]: It obstructs wisdom, causes difficulties, and leads away from nibbāna.

24
00:02:25,868 --> 00:02:28,830
[SPEAKER_01]: When I considered thus, it subsided in me.

25
00:02:30,143 --> 00:02:36,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And whenever a thought of cruelty or ill-will arose in me, I abandoned it, removed it, and did away with it.

26
00:02:40,305 --> 00:02:44,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this... this... obviously, I just want to point out that he was a bodhisattva.

27
00:02:45,066 --> 00:02:48,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone who was inclined towards enlightenment, who is...

28
00:02:49,027 --> 00:02:49,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, towards enlightenment.

29
00:02:50,427 --> 00:02:54,248
[SPEAKER_00]: But you also... and... so, he clearly knew what nibbāna was.

30
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:57,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as I said, it leads away from nibbāna.

31
00:02:57,983 --> 00:03:05,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you already had the understanding of what nibbāna is, and then you decided, what if I divide my thoughts in these two categories?

32
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:14,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, basically, you can also say these two categories is the thoughts that lead away from nibbāna, and the thoughts that don't lead away from nibbāna.

33
00:03:15,255 --> 00:03:20,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts that lead away from nibbāna are thoughts of sensuality, ill-will, and cruelty.

34
00:03:20,919 --> 00:03:24,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts that don't lead away from nibbāna are thoughts of non-sensuality,

35
00:03:25,731 --> 00:03:28,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-ill will and non-quilt of renunciation.

36
00:03:29,753 --> 00:03:46,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nibbāna is something to be understood, which is why understanding brings you closer to it, and then developing your mind in regard to that understanding, basically, makes you sort of abide in nibbāna, like an arahant.

37
00:03:48,566 --> 00:03:54,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm saying this because most, most commonly, nibbāna is understood as some sort of transcendental

38
00:03:55,304 --> 00:04:04,111
[SPEAKER_00]: Out of this world state that you kind of stumble upon, if you have luck, if you've been if you've been trying hard enough, then it happens to you.

39
00:04:04,672 --> 00:04:04,852
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

40
00:04:05,212 --> 00:04:05,953
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basic.

41
00:04:06,013 --> 00:04:07,314
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a practice of understanding.

42
00:04:07,854 --> 00:04:09,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding what nibbāna is.

43
00:04:10,076 --> 00:04:11,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding what dispassion is.

44
00:04:12,038 --> 00:04:13,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it is valuable.

45
00:04:13,519 --> 00:04:14,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the most important thing.

46
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:15,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it is peaceful.

47
00:04:16,221 --> 00:04:17,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it is worthy.

48
00:04:18,383 --> 00:04:19,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And then developing it.

49
00:04:19,964 --> 00:04:20,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing nibbāna.

50
00:04:21,245 --> 00:04:22,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing nibbāna, yes.

51
00:04:25,518 --> 00:04:31,522
[SPEAKER_00]: And another thing to point out there, it's essential thought arose in me.

52
00:04:32,382 --> 00:04:38,526
[SPEAKER_00]: So, essential thought would have arisen in Bodhisattva or in somebody who practices, and he recognized it.

53
00:04:38,606 --> 00:04:39,667
[SPEAKER_00]: It arises on its own.

54
00:04:40,668 --> 00:04:41,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not on him.

55
00:04:42,569 --> 00:04:52,095
[SPEAKER_00]: What is on him is wrongly attending to it, attending it as valuable, attending it as dear, delighting in it, wanting it, proliferating it.

56
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:57,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But turning your mind away from it, then the thing would disappear.

57
00:04:59,332 --> 00:05:02,853
[SPEAKER_00]: So he would see, oh, this is harmful to me.

58
00:05:03,513 --> 00:05:05,293
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not pleasant or agreeable.

59
00:05:07,694 --> 00:05:11,335
[SPEAKER_00]: It leads to my harm, it leads to harm of others, it leads to harm of both.

60
00:05:11,635 --> 00:05:14,997
[SPEAKER_00]: And ultimately, it leads away from nibbāna, which is the ultimate goal.

61
00:05:17,617 --> 00:05:19,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So there would be enough, and then that thought would go away.

62
00:05:21,458 --> 00:05:26,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind would genuinely not want it, which means then it has arisen on its own accord, and then it has ceased.

63
00:05:26,741 --> 00:05:27,001
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

64
00:05:27,021 --> 00:05:32,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it hasn't been... You know, somebody turns up in front of your door, and he's unwelcome, and then he leaves, because he realizes he's unwelcome.

65
00:05:38,387 --> 00:05:39,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it continues.

66
00:05:41,449 --> 00:05:47,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever a bhikkhu frequently thinks and ponders upon, that will become the inclination of his mind.

67
00:05:48,127 --> 00:05:56,353
[SPEAKER_01]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon thoughts of sensual desire, he has abandoned the thought of renunciation to cultivate the thoughts of sensual desire.

68
00:05:57,594 --> 00:06:00,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And then his mind inclines to thoughts of sensual desire.

69
00:06:01,116 --> 00:06:12,124
[SPEAKER_01]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon thoughts of ill-will, upon thoughts of cruelty, he has abandoned the thoughts of non-ill-will or non-cruelty to cultivate the thoughts of cruelty or ill-will.

70
00:06:12,764 --> 00:06:15,366
[SPEAKER_01]: And then his mind inclines to the thoughts of cruelty.

71
00:06:22,710 --> 00:06:36,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as in the last month of the rainy season, in the autumn, when the crops thicken, a cowherd would guard his cows by constantly tapping and poking them on this side and that, with a stick to check and curb them.

72
00:06:37,222 --> 00:06:37,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

73
00:06:38,408 --> 00:06:44,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he sees that he could be flogged, imprisoned, fined or blamed, if he let them stray into the crops.

74
00:06:45,715 --> 00:06:50,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So too, I saw in unwholesome states, danger, degradation and defilement.

75
00:06:51,501 --> 00:06:56,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And in wholesome states, a blessing of renunciation, the aspect of cleanser.

76
00:07:00,838 --> 00:07:07,003
[SPEAKER_01]: As I abided thus, diligent, ardent and resolute, a thought of renunciation arose in me.

77
00:07:07,824 --> 00:07:09,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I understood thus.

78
00:07:09,265 --> 00:07:12,287
[SPEAKER_01]: This thought of renunciation has arisen in me.

79
00:07:12,307 --> 00:07:18,392
[SPEAKER_01]: This does not lead to my own affliction, or to the other's affliction, or to the affliction of both.

80
00:07:19,333 --> 00:07:23,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It aids wisdom, does not cause difficulties, and leads to nibbāna.

81
00:07:25,037 --> 00:07:33,663
[SPEAKER_01]: If I think and ponder upon those thoughts, even for a night, even for a day, even for a night and day, I see nothing to fear from it.

82
00:07:34,523 --> 00:07:38,426
[SPEAKER_01]: But with excessive thinking and pondering, I might tire my body.

83
00:07:39,226 --> 00:07:42,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And when my body is tired, the mind becomes strained.

84
00:07:42,949 --> 00:07:46,711
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the mind is strained, it is far from composure.

85
00:07:48,192 --> 00:07:54,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So I studied my mind internally, quieted it, brought it to singleness, and composed it.

86
00:07:55,024 --> 00:07:55,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

87
00:07:56,245 --> 00:07:58,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So that my mind should not be distrained.

88
00:08:01,451 --> 00:08:06,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Likewise with the thoughts of non-irrelevant thoughts of non-truth.

89
00:08:09,681 --> 00:08:16,008
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, bhikkhus, whatever a bhikkhu frequently thinks and ponders upon, that will become the inclination of his mind.

90
00:08:16,847 --> 00:08:29,811
[SPEAKER_01]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon thoughts of renunciation, he has abandoned thoughts of sensual desire, to cultivate the thought of renunciation, and then his mind inclines to thoughts of renunciation.

91
00:08:29,831 --> 00:08:45,977
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the next simile, just as in the last month of the hot season, when all the crops have been brought inside the village, villages, a cowherd would guard his cows, while staying at the root of a tree, or at, or out in the open,

92
00:08:47,033 --> 00:08:57,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Since he needs only to be mindful that the cows are there, so too there was no need for me... there was need for me only to be mindful that those states were there.

93
00:08:58,543 --> 00:09:04,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Tired... tireless energy was aroused in me and unremitting mindfulness was established.

94
00:09:04,607 --> 00:09:06,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he didn't have to trace them individually.

95
00:09:07,970 --> 00:09:12,434
[SPEAKER_01]: My body was tranquil and untroubled, my mind composed and uniform.

96
00:09:13,935 --> 00:09:16,057
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he goes into the jhānas.

97
00:09:17,837 --> 00:09:24,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, a person needs to know what nibbāna is, which basically means a person needs to develop samādhi, needs to get the right view.

98
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:52,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Once the right view is there, all the person has to do is divide his thoughts into wholesome and unwholesome.

99
00:09:54,206 --> 00:09:58,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Into those that lead away from nibbāna and those that lead towards nibbāna.

100
00:09:58,708 --> 00:10:05,632
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Mahāpajāpati came to the Buddha and said, oh, can you give me a short instruction and I'll go on a retreat deep in the forest.

101
00:10:06,253 --> 00:10:11,056
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, whatever you know it's wholesome, whatever you know it's unwholesome, abandon it.

102
00:10:11,196 --> 00:10:13,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you know it's wholesome, cultivate it.

103
00:10:14,536 --> 00:10:17,418
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of the thinking, and it's exactly the same.

104
00:10:18,019 --> 00:10:22,642
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the right view, all you need to do is divide the thoughts into categories and stick to it.

105
00:10:23,443 --> 00:10:33,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes it's going to be hard work, as much as it is to guard your herd from like, don't go too much left, don't go too much right, don't go into this crop, don't go into that crop, because you'll end up in serious trouble.

106
00:10:34,931 --> 00:10:39,234
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that's done, then all you need to know, herd is there.

107
00:10:40,298 --> 00:10:43,140
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you sit under the tree, on top of a hill, and see it.

108
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:43,660
[SPEAKER_00]: It's there.

109
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:44,201
[SPEAKER_00]: No problem.

110
00:10:44,601 --> 00:10:46,782
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not going anywhere, because there's nothing pulling them now.

111
00:10:47,423 --> 00:10:48,303
[SPEAKER_00]: All the crops are gone.

112
00:10:49,244 --> 00:10:55,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they're just, just, just, just kind of at ease, sitting on a field, and you can see it.

113
00:10:55,248 --> 00:10:57,809
[SPEAKER_00]: And that means, oh, you're not... you have so much energy now.

114
00:10:58,370 --> 00:10:58,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Such a peace.

115
00:10:59,090 --> 00:11:02,893
[SPEAKER_00]: No worry, no distraction, not tired.

116
00:11:03,785 --> 00:11:06,366
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're established upon it, and that is the pleasure of jhāna.

117
00:11:06,626 --> 00:11:10,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, for instance, the thoughts of renunciation, you don't have to be cultivating...

118
00:11:10,847 --> 00:11:11,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

119
00:11:11,267 --> 00:11:12,188
[SPEAKER_01]: ...thoughts of renunciation.

120
00:11:12,248 --> 00:11:12,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now I get it.

121
00:11:12,928 --> 00:11:18,730
[SPEAKER_00]: The cultivation basically comes down to saying no to sensuality, saying no to a will, saying no to cruelty.

122
00:11:18,830 --> 00:11:22,851
[SPEAKER_00]: When you get established upon that, it's just there, and that is effortless.

123
00:11:23,592 --> 00:11:30,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And knowing that, then this whole joy and the unremitting mindfulness and tireless energy arises.

124
00:11:31,848 --> 00:11:32,909
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the first jhāna.

125
00:11:33,409 --> 00:11:34,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the thoughts are there.

126
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:40,674
[SPEAKER_00]: The thoughts that are not thoughts of sensuality, not thoughts of ill-will, not thoughts of corporate.

127
00:11:43,176 --> 00:11:48,199
[SPEAKER_00]: And by having just these thoughts there, you know, ah, those unwholesome thoughts cannot enter.

128
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:49,120
[SPEAKER_00]: They're gone.

129
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:50,461
[SPEAKER_00]: They're dispelled.

130
00:11:52,922 --> 00:11:58,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they said, usually, people were trying to cultivate thoughts of kindness.

131
00:12:00,937 --> 00:12:05,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he actually just just kind of gave me another point to say.

132
00:12:05,561 --> 00:12:19,353
[SPEAKER_00]: People who try to cultivate practice of jhāna, inverted commas, sought to then not have sensuality and not have this, but that needs to be abandoned beforehand, through wisdom and clear seeing, and then you enter the jhāna.

133
00:12:21,383 --> 00:12:31,866
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when these, like, quite secluded from unwholesome states, as every description of jhāna says, then the mind gets settled in that, established, and then he enters and abides in that jhāna.

134
00:12:33,286 --> 00:12:39,628
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was saying earlier today, then he can, once his mind is abiding in jhāna, then that's another sutta.

135
00:12:40,068 --> 00:12:45,829
[SPEAKER_00]: He walks around, he urinates, he defecates, he extends his arm, he does this, and his mind is in that jhāna.

136
00:12:46,929 --> 00:12:48,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's secluded from unwholesome states.

137
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:51,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Like deer in a mosquito-free marsh.

138
00:12:52,421 --> 00:12:55,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, or like monks in a mosquito-free marsh.

139
00:12:55,561 --> 00:12:57,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Like right here.

140
00:12:57,662 --> 00:13:12,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is then, once such level of uninterrupted clarity is established, with the right view, which means with knowledge of what nibbāna is, then the mind gets developed knowledge, further and further and further, as that sukha will go.

141
00:13:13,266 --> 00:13:16,667
[SPEAKER_00]: And that enters and abides in nibbāna and meets finish.

142
00:13:16,867 --> 00:13:19,728
[SPEAKER_00]: No more asuras to come back, and it's done.

143
00:13:30,074 --> 00:13:31,755
[SPEAKER_00]: In nibbāna, it's not hard to understand.

144
00:13:33,815 --> 00:13:37,556
[SPEAKER_00]: It's subtle, because people's minds are too far away from it.

145
00:13:38,357 --> 00:13:42,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they revolve around sensuality, ill-will and cruelty.

146
00:13:43,518 --> 00:13:46,719
[SPEAKER_00]: But nibbāna literally means absence of greed, aversion, delusion.

147
00:13:47,299 --> 00:13:51,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It means absence of passion, cooling down of all the passions you can imagine.

148
00:13:51,801 --> 00:13:52,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Subtle, of course.

149
00:13:53,381 --> 00:13:55,422
[SPEAKER_00]: See, even Pūrṭujā can get his head around that.

150
00:13:56,942 --> 00:13:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And if he was to stick to it,

151
00:13:59,581 --> 00:14:13,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Stick to avoiding unwholesome, stick to virtue, stick to sīla, stick to all of those other things that would be conducive towards his idea of dispassion, then he will actually clarify that idea more and more.

152
00:14:14,133 --> 00:14:27,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And with further reading of the suttas and input from from the Buddha or from if he has a living arahant or a living sotāpanna, even better, but even without it, he would actually come close and then, yeah, that's what nibbāna is and get the right view.

153
00:14:30,203 --> 00:14:41,771
[SPEAKER_00]: But it will require, obviously, quite a great deal of mind development, of sticking to the, you know, not, not, not letting your, your, your mental thoughts, your, your herd wander into the crops.

154
00:14:43,012 --> 00:14:44,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean saying no to everything.

155
00:14:44,473 --> 00:14:50,937
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to say no to things if it doesn't involve sensuality, if it doesn't involve cruelty, if it doesn't involve real world.

156
00:14:52,067 --> 00:14:59,214
[SPEAKER_00]: But you certainly need to be very mindful of it, because it might involve sensuality, it might involve the world, it might involve the whole world.

157
00:14:59,234 --> 00:15:03,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I always bring affliction to myself, to others, does it?

158
00:15:03,118 --> 00:15:03,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it?

159
00:15:04,039 --> 00:15:06,801
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it doesn't bring affliction to me, not to others, not to both.

160
00:15:07,302 --> 00:15:10,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay then, but even then, you don't eat negligently.

161
00:15:11,226 --> 00:15:13,288
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't give in to it with negligence.

162
00:15:13,468 --> 00:15:15,670
[SPEAKER_01]: But with, like, sensual dissolve?

163
00:15:17,008 --> 00:15:21,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexual intercourse, you say, will this bring affliction to myself?

164
00:15:22,253 --> 00:15:24,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like the other sutta, that is to be completely abandoned.

165
00:15:25,075 --> 00:15:33,001
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no ground whereby sexual intercourse or sensuality of that kind would, oh, can be done mindfully or something like that.

166
00:15:33,041 --> 00:15:33,461
[SPEAKER_00]: We can't.

167
00:15:34,382 --> 00:15:43,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can eat mindfully, you can sort of come across sight, sound, smell, taste, touch mindfully, but sexual intercourse cannot be done mindfully.

168
00:15:44,890 --> 00:15:46,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that needs to be abandoned.

169
00:15:52,372 --> 00:15:57,536
[SPEAKER_01]: Entanglement.

170
00:15:57,556 --> 00:15:58,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Difficulties.

171
00:15:59,697 --> 00:16:09,564
[SPEAKER_00]: And fundamentally, as I said before, as the suttas said before, the one we read, you can't have sensuality without being bound by sensual perceptions.

172
00:16:10,385 --> 00:16:16,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, you can't engage in a sexual intercourse without being bound by sensual perceptions.

173
00:16:17,549 --> 00:16:21,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't say, I'm free from sensuality and then practice sexual intercourse.

174
00:16:22,472 --> 00:16:22,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

175
00:16:24,013 --> 00:16:28,095
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no sexual intercourse without your mind being bound by sensual perceptions.

176
00:16:29,255 --> 00:16:33,257
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind is bound by sensual perceptions, well, that is as far from nibbāna as it can be.

177
00:16:42,921 --> 00:16:45,362
[SPEAKER_01]: The sūtra ends with one of my favorite things.

178
00:16:46,163 --> 00:16:51,625
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says, there are these roots of trees, these empty huts,

179
00:16:52,507 --> 00:16:56,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Meditate, Bhikkhus, do not delay, or else you will regret it later.

180
00:16:58,490 --> 00:17:11,115
[SPEAKER_00]: So, Bhikkhus, you have the right view, you know what nibbāna is, now go to these empty dwellings, roots of trees, forests, mountains, and practice, develop further clarity.

181
00:17:14,096 --> 00:17:14,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't be negligent.

182
00:17:17,497 --> 00:17:18,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you've always got it.

183
00:17:20,752 --> 00:17:27,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So that sutta ends, and that is what the Blessed One said, because we're satisfied and delighted in the Blessed One's words.

184
00:17:51,347 --> 00:17:59,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's another sutta on the same subject of thoughts.

185
00:18:00,851 --> 00:18:04,532
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the next one, Madhyamakāya's.

186
00:18:06,253 --> 00:18:06,473
[SPEAKER_01]: 20.

187
00:18:07,674 --> 00:18:09,314
[SPEAKER_01]: The Removal of Distracting Thoughts.

188
00:18:11,675 --> 00:18:15,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus have I heard, on one occasion, that Blessed One was living in Sarvatthi.

189
00:18:16,157 --> 00:18:17,577
[SPEAKER_01]: There He addressed the bhikkhus thus.

190
00:18:18,278 --> 00:18:18,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhikkhus.

191
00:18:19,551 --> 00:18:22,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Venerable Sir David Blunt, the Blessed One, said this.

192
00:18:24,253 --> 00:18:31,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, when a bhikkhu is pursuing the higher mind, from time to time, he should give attention to five signs.

193
00:18:32,215 --> 00:18:33,055
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the five?

194
00:18:35,396 --> 00:18:47,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because, when a bhikkhu is giving attention to some sign, and owing to that sign, there arise in him evil, unwholesome thoughts, connected with desire, connected with hate,

195
00:18:48,151 --> 00:18:53,834
[SPEAKER_01]: With delusion, then you should give attention to some other song connected with what is wholesome.

196
00:18:55,015 --> 00:19:06,721
[SPEAKER_01]: When he gives attention to some other sign connected with what is wholesome, then any evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate, and with the delusion are abandoned in him and subside.

197
00:19:07,842 --> 00:19:15,046
[SPEAKER_01]: With the abandoning of them, his mind becomes steady internally, quieted, brought to singleness and composed.

198
00:19:16,159 --> 00:19:37,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a skilled carpenter or his apprentice might knock out, remove, or extract a coarse peg by means of a fine yarn, so too, when a bhikkhu gives attention to some other sound connected with what is wholesome, his mind becomes steadied, internally bighted, brought to singleness, and concludes.

199
00:19:42,069 --> 00:19:44,751
[SPEAKER_01]: If while he's giving attention... Let me just address...

200
00:19:47,308 --> 00:19:53,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, obviously, the main thing that emerges from this, it's understanding the nature of the sign.

201
00:19:54,652 --> 00:19:57,354
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind arises on its own.

202
00:19:57,594 --> 00:19:58,275
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a phenomenon.

203
00:19:58,595 --> 00:19:59,475
[SPEAKER_00]: It's manifested.

204
00:20:00,956 --> 00:20:02,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You attend to its aspects.

205
00:20:03,618 --> 00:20:08,781
[SPEAKER_00]: And based on the aspects you're attending, based on the signs, signs and tokens, as often we say, in the circles.

206
00:20:09,821 --> 00:20:14,524
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's like a general notion in regard to the arisen mind, to the arisen thought.

207
00:20:16,458 --> 00:20:24,743
[SPEAKER_00]: By attending to them, then you might be contributing towards more of the unwholesome thoughts, or towards going back to the wholesome thoughts, depending on what you choose to attend to.

208
00:20:26,104 --> 00:20:41,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is to recognize it's... it has like a slightly indirect nature, in a sense that you're not trying to deny what has arisen in your mind, or you're trying to deny what mood your mind is in, what the general state mind is in.

209
00:20:41,253 --> 00:20:42,294
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not denying that.

210
00:20:42,992 --> 00:20:48,697
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just recognizing that by attending to something else, it will drift away from that, if that was unwholesome.

211
00:20:49,137 --> 00:20:50,638
[SPEAKER_00]: If that was wholesome, then it's fine.

212
00:20:51,479 --> 00:20:54,822
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep your watch on it, so to speak.

213
00:20:54,882 --> 00:20:58,985
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it's wholesome, that doesn't mean you become negligent, as I said just a minute ago.

214
00:20:59,586 --> 00:21:03,429
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep watching, but it's... now it's effortless, because you don't need to take it away from you.

215
00:21:04,230 --> 00:21:10,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... it's... it's like... like a... like a... again, a simile of a cow herd, that you have to keep an eye on.

216
00:21:11,311 --> 00:21:20,439
[SPEAKER_00]: If the cows see the green crops, well, they will be very inclined towards... then you have to hold them back, and that's hard work, and it's tiresome.

217
00:21:20,840 --> 00:21:24,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you then foresee the sign of the green crops.

218
00:21:24,623 --> 00:21:26,965
[SPEAKER_00]: They will disturb, they will excite the cows.

219
00:21:27,726 --> 00:21:35,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you direct them somewhere else, in a different direction, where they won't see the green crops, and then they'll be calm, because there's nothing exciting it.

220
00:21:36,448 --> 00:21:41,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll be at ease, because you don't have to work hard to keep it away from the crocs.

221
00:21:42,170 --> 00:21:43,571
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just as simple as turning to something else.

222
00:21:43,611 --> 00:21:44,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

223
00:21:44,071 --> 00:21:49,713
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize, by being in such an environment, or by thinking certain things, well, the mind will get excited.

224
00:21:50,613 --> 00:21:51,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't do that.

225
00:21:51,553 --> 00:21:52,994
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at the opposite.

226
00:21:53,054 --> 00:21:55,955
[SPEAKER_00]: You focus on the opposite, so then the mind calms down.

227
00:21:57,115 --> 00:21:59,656
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's important to have that sign of that mind.

228
00:22:00,437 --> 00:22:01,817
[SPEAKER_00]: To recognize it for what it is.

229
00:22:02,977 --> 00:22:04,358
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an independent animal.

230
00:22:06,405 --> 00:22:10,348
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not you, because that would imply that, like, direction.

231
00:22:10,388 --> 00:22:14,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sorry, direct kind of control of it, which you don't have.

232
00:22:15,691 --> 00:22:16,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It has a reason.

233
00:22:16,992 --> 00:22:18,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have indirect responsibility.

234
00:22:18,733 --> 00:22:25,257
[SPEAKER_00]: By bringing your cowherd foolishly in front of a green crop, what do you expect is going to happen?

235
00:22:27,078 --> 00:22:29,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you foresee that, and you don't put them there.

236
00:22:29,700 --> 00:22:32,422
[SPEAKER_00]: You put them somewhere else, maybe behind the corner, where they can't see it.

237
00:22:33,977 --> 00:22:34,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they might smell it.

238
00:22:35,517 --> 00:22:37,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Every now and then, if the wind blows in their direction.

239
00:22:37,858 --> 00:22:40,698
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you refine the signs and tokens you pick on.

240
00:22:41,098 --> 00:22:43,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, they don't see it, but if the wind blows, they smell it.

241
00:22:43,459 --> 00:22:45,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you do it in a different way.

242
00:22:45,479 --> 00:22:50,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you block the smell, or you light the fire, so the smoke kind of obscures the smell, or various things.

243
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:57,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Point being, you recognize the mind as an independent animal that needs kind of catering and watchful eye.

244
00:22:58,082 --> 00:22:58,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, true.

245
00:22:59,742 --> 00:23:03,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you take it away from sensuality and bring it closer to nibbāna.

246
00:23:06,906 --> 00:23:07,827
[SPEAKER_01]: The sutta continues.

247
00:23:09,048 --> 00:23:23,304
[SPEAKER_01]: If while he's giving attention to some other sign connected with what is wholesome, there still arise in him evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate and with delusion, then he should examine the danger in those thoughts.

248
00:23:24,465 --> 00:23:25,707
[SPEAKER_01]: These thoughts are unwholesome.

249
00:23:26,047 --> 00:23:27,088
[SPEAKER_01]: They are reprehensible.

250
00:23:27,248 --> 00:23:28,630
[SPEAKER_01]: They result in suffering.

251
00:23:29,538 --> 00:23:39,060
[SPEAKER_01]: When he examines the danger in those thoughts, then any evil, unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hates and with delusion are abandoned in him and subside.

252
00:23:39,861 --> 00:23:46,682
[SPEAKER_01]: With the abandoning of them, his mind becomes steadied internally, quieted, brought to singleness and concentrated.

253
00:23:47,543 --> 00:23:48,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a man or a woman

254
00:23:50,103 --> 00:23:53,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when simply directing away from whatever was unwholesome is not enough,

255
00:24:20,633 --> 00:24:22,574
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you have to make more of an effort.

256
00:24:24,796 --> 00:24:28,839
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, you start dwelling on the dangers of letting cow go into the crop.

257
00:24:29,739 --> 00:24:35,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Imprisonment, trouble, difficulty, fine, basically, the dangerous sensuality.

258
00:24:36,684 --> 00:24:45,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, like, gives you more, well, more motivation to take it away from that which, which excites it.

259
00:24:45,770 --> 00:24:48,052
[SPEAKER_00]: This will take your mind away to bring it to dispassion.

260
00:24:48,132 --> 00:24:48,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

261
00:24:55,767 --> 00:25:08,033
[SPEAKER_01]: If, while he is examining the danger in those thoughts, there still arises in him evil and wholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate and with delusions, then he should try to forget those thoughts and should not give attention to them.

262
00:25:08,966 --> 00:25:20,036
[SPEAKER_01]: When he tries to forget those thoughts and does not give attention to them, then any evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate, and with delusion, are abandoned in him and sapsang.

263
00:25:21,217 --> 00:25:26,582
[SPEAKER_01]: With the abandoning of them, his mind becomes steadied internally, quieted, broad, synchronous, and composed.

264
00:25:27,623 --> 00:25:35,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a man with good eyes, who did not want to see forms that had come within range of sight, would either shut his eyes or look away.

265
00:25:36,407 --> 00:25:45,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So too, when a beggar tries to forget those thoughts and does not give attention to them, his mind becomes steadied, internally quieted, brought to singleness and composure.

266
00:25:46,709 --> 00:25:49,830
[SPEAKER_00]: So, back to the using the cow simile, cow head.

267
00:25:50,570 --> 00:25:55,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you've taken the cow away from the... from seeing the crop.

268
00:25:58,392 --> 00:26:02,552
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no smell of the... of the... of the fresh grass, fresh crop, disturbing them anymore.

269
00:26:04,273 --> 00:26:05,613
[SPEAKER_00]: They're tied down to the ground.

270
00:26:06,932 --> 00:26:12,175
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep an eye on them, but basically, they're still excited, because they still have the memory of the crop.

271
00:26:12,655 --> 00:26:18,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you just sit down and ignore them, because you know, well, they can't get there, because you took them away.

272
00:26:19,360 --> 00:26:21,641
[SPEAKER_00]: They're secured wherever they are away from the crop.

273
00:26:22,682 --> 00:26:25,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ignore them, because they will calm down, and then they calm down.

274
00:26:25,603 --> 00:26:27,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't fuss around, because it disturbs you.

275
00:26:28,045 --> 00:26:33,268
[SPEAKER_00]: You recognize, okay, you needed to fuss around until there was still a danger of them going into the crop.

276
00:26:33,938 --> 00:26:38,942
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you've done, you removed that that possibility, now just ignore it and they'll come down.

277
00:26:40,583 --> 00:26:43,005
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like being in a secluded little forest.

278
00:26:43,025 --> 00:26:43,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

279
00:26:44,026 --> 00:26:48,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Like foreseeing they're going into the forest, there won't be any sensual disturbances.

280
00:26:49,890 --> 00:26:50,651
[SPEAKER_00]: You go to a forest.

281
00:26:50,731 --> 00:26:55,415
[SPEAKER_00]: You take your mind into the forest by basically walking your body there.

282
00:26:57,451 --> 00:27:00,895
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you leave your mind, your body there, which means your mind is bound with it.

283
00:27:00,915 --> 00:27:03,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your mind has to be in that forest, in that environment.

284
00:27:04,038 --> 00:27:06,220
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it will calm down.

285
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:13,007
[SPEAKER_00]: There might be memory or the perceptions of excitement or whatever else.

286
00:27:14,068 --> 00:27:17,171
[SPEAKER_00]: But sooner or later, it calms down.

287
00:27:17,251 --> 00:27:20,374
[SPEAKER_00]: It forgets about.

288
00:27:24,394 --> 00:27:40,160
[SPEAKER_01]: If while he is trying to forget those thoughts and is not giving attention to them, there still arise in him evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate and with delusion, then he should give attention to the stilling of the thought determination of those thoughts.

289
00:27:42,001 --> 00:27:51,505
[SPEAKER_01]: When he gives attention to the stilling of the thought determination of those thoughts, then any evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate and with delusion are abandoned in him and subside.

290
00:28:01,989 --> 00:28:06,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a man walking fast might consider, why am I walking fast?

291
00:28:07,070 --> 00:28:08,051
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I walk slowly?

292
00:28:08,871 --> 00:28:10,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he... he would walk slowly.

293
00:28:11,031 --> 00:28:13,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he might consider, why am I walking slowly?

294
00:28:13,852 --> 00:28:14,652
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I stand?

295
00:28:15,652 --> 00:28:16,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he would stand.

296
00:28:16,953 --> 00:28:19,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he might consider, why am I standing?

297
00:28:19,513 --> 00:28:20,214
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I sit?

298
00:28:20,734 --> 00:28:21,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he would sit.

299
00:28:22,283 --> 00:28:24,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he might consider, why am I sitting?

300
00:28:24,305 --> 00:28:26,067
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I lay down?

301
00:28:26,087 --> 00:28:26,868
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would lay down.

302
00:28:28,109 --> 00:28:33,234
[SPEAKER_01]: By doing so, he would substitute for each grosser posture, one that was subtler.

303
00:28:33,455 --> 00:28:33,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

304
00:28:35,677 --> 00:28:44,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that point, or that step, so to speak, basically, that's the step that goes beyond wholesome and unwholesome.

305
00:28:45,188 --> 00:28:45,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

306
00:28:45,448 --> 00:28:51,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Because addressing unwholesome, addressing dangerous with wholesome, didn't work.

307
00:28:52,150 --> 00:28:53,311
[SPEAKER_00]: The threat is still there.

308
00:28:54,531 --> 00:28:58,332
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that step pertains to the nature of thoughts.

309
00:28:59,533 --> 00:29:02,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Stealing that which determines the thinking.

310
00:29:04,915 --> 00:29:10,597
[SPEAKER_00]: That which every thought has in common, be it wholesome or unwholesome.

311
00:29:10,637 --> 00:29:14,478
[SPEAKER_00]: And what every thought has in common is movement as a phenomenon.

312
00:29:18,253 --> 00:29:21,636
[SPEAKER_00]: On a level of images, of mind, there is a movement.

313
00:29:22,496 --> 00:29:28,101
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize, okay, they want to move in this direction, I take them, I move them into this direction.

314
00:29:28,161 --> 00:29:33,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, they want to move, they still want to move the other direction, then I ignore the movement, then I do this.

315
00:29:33,425 --> 00:29:38,489
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when none of that works, then you start refining the nature of the movement.

316
00:29:39,109 --> 00:29:41,091
[SPEAKER_00]: As a result of that, the course

317
00:29:42,542 --> 00:29:51,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Movement that's being... sorry, the core sensuality that's being carried with a coarser form of movement is being sieved out.

318
00:29:52,244 --> 00:30:04,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're walking fast, which is what sensuality is, basically excitement, you slow down and then there is no room through that refined movement for a coarser movement of sensuality to come through.

319
00:30:04,887 --> 00:30:09,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you refine it further and further and further until it's still and it doesn't move.

320
00:30:14,181 --> 00:30:20,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no sensuality, there is no disturbance, there is no excitement without mental movement.

321
00:30:20,947 --> 00:30:23,970
[SPEAKER_00]: There can be a mental movement without that particular excitement.

322
00:30:24,130 --> 00:30:32,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you use mental movement, the refining of the mental movement, so to abandon that particular unwholesome excitement.

323
00:30:33,218 --> 00:30:37,182
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the mind comes out of that singleness, that stillness,

324
00:30:38,285 --> 00:30:44,768
[SPEAKER_00]: But the excitement is gone, and then you can go back to the normal, whatever, daily movement of a wholesome thought.

325
00:30:48,289 --> 00:30:52,631
[SPEAKER_01]: To do this, you obviously have to be able to see your mind still there.

326
00:30:53,152 --> 00:30:58,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Even to do the first step, you need to have the sign of your mind, which means you need to have the right view again.

327
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:02,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that section sounds like...

328
00:31:02,901 --> 00:31:08,042
[SPEAKER_01]: I've heard others explain it, that one should think slowly.

329
00:31:08,742 --> 00:31:14,063
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, like, I am going to get up...

330
00:31:15,603 --> 00:31:25,025
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, you are kind of slowing down your... Well, you're refining your thoughts, you're slowing down the movement of your thoughts, but you're not slowing down your discernment or your clarity.

331
00:31:28,228 --> 00:31:31,651
[SPEAKER_01]: You're slowing down the intentions?

332
00:31:32,812 --> 00:31:35,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Not necessarily.

333
00:31:35,615 --> 00:31:37,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention is to get rid of the unwholesome.

334
00:31:38,958 --> 00:31:40,219
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still very much present.

335
00:31:40,819 --> 00:31:41,900
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're refining.

336
00:31:41,920 --> 00:31:44,042
[SPEAKER_00]: You're basically seeing unwholesome.

337
00:31:44,923 --> 00:31:46,484
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the unwholesome thought.

338
00:31:47,225 --> 00:31:48,746
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a thought.

339
00:31:49,647 --> 00:31:55,112
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a movement within that thought that's dictated by that unwholesome, basically.

340
00:31:56,031 --> 00:31:57,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can say like this.

341
00:31:57,412 --> 00:31:59,713
[SPEAKER_00]: There is an unwholesome moving thought.

342
00:32:01,454 --> 00:32:03,155
[SPEAKER_00]: Refine that there is a moving thought.

343
00:32:04,115 --> 00:32:10,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Refine that there is a thought, still thought, that doesn't move.

344
00:32:14,821 --> 00:32:16,282
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start seeing

345
00:32:17,277 --> 00:32:23,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the movement of the unwholesome thought, and then within that, and then within that, and then within that.

346
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:30,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And that stillness, and that's quite... requires clarity, and a clear notion of the sign of the mind, sign of the thought.

347
00:32:30,784 --> 00:32:39,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not saying to, like, slow down your thought, but actually, just discern a more general

348
00:32:40,928 --> 00:32:42,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Phenomenon, basically.

349
00:32:42,229 --> 00:32:42,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

350
00:32:42,790 --> 00:32:43,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that thought.

351
00:32:44,271 --> 00:32:47,113
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what really pertains to the nature of the thought.

352
00:32:47,373 --> 00:32:47,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

353
00:32:47,953 --> 00:32:55,779
[SPEAKER_00]: The unwholesome moving thought, wholesome moving thought, they share the... in a particular aspect, they share the same nature of a moving thought.

354
00:32:58,160 --> 00:33:05,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, moving thought of the... pertaining to unwholesome, moving thought pertaining to wholesome, you can take it further.

355
00:33:05,705 --> 00:33:08,127
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... what do they have in common?

356
00:33:08,147 --> 00:33:09,208
[SPEAKER_00]: They are moving thoughts.

357
00:33:12,278 --> 00:33:16,081
[SPEAKER_00]: What does any moving thought, no matter of the movement, has in common?

358
00:33:17,403 --> 00:33:18,403
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a thought.

359
00:33:19,404 --> 00:33:24,128
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're saying, you're looking at a subtler...

360
00:33:24,769 --> 00:33:25,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, that's what it says.

361
00:33:26,110 --> 00:33:26,751
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm walking fast.

362
00:33:26,771 --> 00:33:29,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I walking... Why is this body being moving fast?

363
00:33:30,154 --> 00:33:31,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't I slow it down?

364
00:33:31,735 --> 00:33:32,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't I stand?

365
00:33:33,957 --> 00:33:37,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Why don't I have the body in a non-moving position?

366
00:33:38,582 --> 00:33:40,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Not running fast, start walking slowly.

367
00:33:40,783 --> 00:33:42,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Not walking slowly, standing.

368
00:33:42,223 --> 00:33:43,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Not standing, sitting down.

369
00:33:44,004 --> 00:33:45,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Not sitting down, laying down.

370
00:33:45,964 --> 00:33:48,625
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what all of those postures have in common?

371
00:33:48,645 --> 00:33:49,345
[SPEAKER_00]: They're all body.

372
00:33:50,385 --> 00:33:51,486
[SPEAKER_00]: They all pertain to the body.

373
00:33:51,966 --> 00:33:55,927
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're refining in terms of the movement, in terms of the engagement.

374
00:33:56,867 --> 00:33:58,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Subtler and subtler and subtler.

375
00:34:02,057 --> 00:34:10,960
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the thread of the clarity of pertaining to the thought, or pertaining to the mind, or pertaining to the intent of abandoning unwholesome, remains.

376
00:34:11,160 --> 00:34:12,901
[SPEAKER_00]: That thread stays through all of that.

377
00:34:15,322 --> 00:34:21,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, you use it to abandon the unwholesome movement of your thought.

378
00:34:24,485 --> 00:34:28,307
[SPEAKER_00]: When nothing else worked, you use it, basically, until

379
00:34:29,337 --> 00:34:36,301
[SPEAKER_00]: The unwholesome falls off, and then, okay, now you can start moving again, if you want, if there is a need for it.

380
00:34:40,023 --> 00:34:40,644
[SPEAKER_01]: It continues.

381
00:34:41,624 --> 00:34:51,710
[SPEAKER_01]: If while he is giving attention to the spinning of the thought determination of those thoughts, there still arise in him evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hates, and with delusion,

382
00:34:52,507 --> 00:35:01,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, with his teeth clenched and his tongue pressed against the roof of his mouth, he should beat down, constrain and crush mind with mind.

383
00:35:02,269 --> 00:35:17,432
[SPEAKER_01]: When, with his teeth clenched and his tongue pressed against the roof of his mouth, he beats down, constrains and crushes mind with mind, then any any evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate and with delusion are abandoned in him and subside.

384
00:35:18,252 --> 00:35:23,835
[SPEAKER_01]: With the abandoning of them, his mind becomes steadied, internally quieted, brought to singleness and composed.

385
00:35:24,776 --> 00:35:41,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as a strong man might seize a weaker man by the head or the shoulders and beat him down, constrain him and crush him, so too, when with his teeth clenched and his tongue pressed against the roof of his mouth, a bhikkhu beats down, constrains and crushes mind with mind.

386
00:35:42,287 --> 00:35:47,290
[SPEAKER_01]: His mind becomes steadied, internally quieted, brought to singleness and composed.

387
00:35:49,892 --> 00:35:50,912
[SPEAKER_01]: That's quite a false view.

388
00:35:51,353 --> 00:35:51,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

389
00:35:51,813 --> 00:35:56,654
[SPEAKER_00]: When nothing else works, you still refuse to allow cowherd to go into that crop.

390
00:35:57,895 --> 00:36:08,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, no matter how hard it is, it's still not as hard as then, enduring the punishment, once they've got gone into the crop.

391
00:36:09,279 --> 00:36:15,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have to, you then just basically put a rope and just, just, just pin them down until they come down.

392
00:36:17,070 --> 00:36:20,632
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will exhaust you, but still the purpose will be fulfilled.

393
00:36:21,312 --> 00:36:23,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the cowherd away from the green fox.

394
00:36:24,954 --> 00:36:26,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And it has to calm down.

395
00:36:26,515 --> 00:36:28,836
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't... they themselves will tire out.

396
00:36:29,416 --> 00:36:34,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just make... make your determination stronger, longer lasting than their determination.

397
00:36:35,663 --> 00:36:37,904
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite a simple thing to understand it.

398
00:36:38,124 --> 00:36:40,145
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can see this is unwholesome.

399
00:36:40,285 --> 00:36:40,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

400
00:36:40,765 --> 00:36:40,966
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

401
00:36:41,326 --> 00:36:41,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

402
00:36:41,906 --> 00:36:42,646
[SPEAKER_01]: And you just stop.

403
00:36:42,786 --> 00:36:42,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

404
00:36:43,407 --> 00:36:44,027
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

405
00:36:44,187 --> 00:36:59,755
[SPEAKER_00]: But what people don't understand then, the pain, the sharp pain that will come, seemingly, because of the restraint, the fear, the panic, the neurosis, everything else that comes, that's all part of that cow's herd rebellion.

406
00:37:00,475 --> 00:37:02,116
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all part of the weaker man's...

407
00:37:02,876 --> 00:37:04,657
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you don't know he's a weaker man.

408
00:37:06,304 --> 00:37:10,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Until here comes down, based on your grip, then you know you're stronger.

409
00:37:11,389 --> 00:37:13,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you'll find out you're stronger, if you prevail.

410
00:37:21,458 --> 00:37:31,527
[SPEAKER_00]: But people usually, when it comes down to restraint, and then the pain comes, and then the fear,

411
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:40,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Various emotions now being caused by... you become more sensitive or whatever else, then people start developing lots of views on account of that.

412
00:37:41,747 --> 00:37:46,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Views that take them away from a quite simple, this is wholesome, this is unwholesome.

413
00:37:50,713 --> 00:37:52,815
[SPEAKER_00]: You start justifying or you start giving in.

414
00:37:55,297 --> 00:37:56,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do need the right view.

415
00:37:57,581 --> 00:38:03,782
[SPEAKER_00]: To not give in to that, because anybody can practice restraint, but restraint in itself does not lead to the right view.

416
00:38:04,803 --> 00:38:10,044
[SPEAKER_00]: It aids the right view to fulfillment, but it cannot bring you the right view by itself.

417
00:38:14,145 --> 00:38:20,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, if you don't know what nibbāna is, you can't possibly know what comes closer to it, what drifts away from it.

418
00:38:22,857 --> 00:38:32,201
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you clarify as much as you can with what nibbāna is, and then you start emulating the behavior, mental states, actions of that which is in nibbāna.

419
00:38:34,522 --> 00:38:35,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting like an arahant.

420
00:38:35,782 --> 00:38:37,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Acting like an arahant, exactly.

421
00:38:37,063 --> 00:38:40,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, well, I'm not an arahant yet, so I can't do... No, you can't.

422
00:38:42,905 --> 00:38:43,346
[SPEAKER_00]: If you want to...

423
00:38:43,406 --> 00:38:46,227
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not an arahant yet, means you need to act like an arahant harder.

424
00:38:49,468 --> 00:38:51,309
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, you know, it might break somebody.

425
00:38:54,713 --> 00:38:56,614
[SPEAKER_00]: I want people up to such a point that they crack.

426
00:38:57,455 --> 00:38:58,455
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the real risk.

427
00:38:59,756 --> 00:39:02,097
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hard to say, like, act like an Arahant.

428
00:39:02,137 --> 00:39:02,677
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

429
00:39:02,898 --> 00:39:04,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it doesn't mean act like an Arahant.

430
00:39:04,438 --> 00:39:05,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Act in front of others.

431
00:39:05,619 --> 00:39:08,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, like, don't tolerate anything unwholesome in your mind.

432
00:39:08,961 --> 00:39:13,283
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't tolerate anything unwholesome in your mind, you won't be able to engage in anything unwholesome by your speech.

433
00:39:13,944 --> 00:39:18,066
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can't engage in anything unwholesome with your speech, you can't do anything unwholesome with your body.

434
00:39:19,146 --> 00:39:22,548
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean go around, you know, acting loftier than everybody

435
00:39:24,056 --> 00:39:24,216
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

436
00:39:24,376 --> 00:39:27,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Act like an arahant means emulate arahant in your thoughts.

437
00:39:27,358 --> 00:39:27,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

438
00:39:27,998 --> 00:39:28,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

439
00:39:29,058 --> 00:39:32,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

440
00:39:32,740 --> 00:39:34,201
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is the ending of the sutta.

441
00:39:35,662 --> 00:39:43,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when a bhikkhu is giving attention to some sign and only to that sign there arise in him evil unwholesome thoughts connected with desire, with hate, and with delusion.

442
00:39:45,006 --> 00:39:52,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when he gives attention to some other sign connected with what is wholesome, any such evil unwholesome thoughts are abandoned in him and subside.

443
00:39:53,787 --> 00:40:10,273
[SPEAKER_01]: When he examines the danger in those thoughts, when he tries to forget those thoughts and does not give attention to them, when he gives attention to the stilling of the thought determination of those thoughts,

444
00:40:11,777 --> 00:40:29,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, when, with his teeth clenched and his tongue pressed against the roof of his mouth, he beats down, constrains and flushes mind with mind, any such evil thoughts, unwholesome thoughts are abandoned in him, and his mind becomes steadied, internally quieted, brought to singleness and composed.

445
00:40:30,630 --> 00:40:34,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, this bhikkhu is then called the master of the courses of thought.

446
00:40:35,653 --> 00:40:41,477
[SPEAKER_01]: He will think whatever thought he wishes to think, and he will not think any thoughts that he does not wish to think.

447
00:40:42,277 --> 00:40:49,762
[SPEAKER_01]: He has severed the craving, flung off the fetters, and with the complete penetration of the conceit, he has made an end of suffering.

448
00:40:50,542 --> 00:40:55,446
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what the Blessed One said, and the bhikkhus were satisfied and delighted in the Blessed One's words.

